<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nerio I Acciaioli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">?–1394</span> · Also known as Nerio I Acciajuoli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nerio I Acciaioli was a Florentine noble who made himself Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. From his base at Corinth he took Athens from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-company">Catalan Company</a> in 1388, ending Catalan rule, and was recognised as duke by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a> in 1394, shortly before his death. After a period of Venetian rule, his family held the duchy until the Ottoman conquest in the 1450s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nerio-i-acciaioli.jpg" alt="Nerio I Acciaioli" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Fanelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neri_Acciaiuoli_primo_Duca_d%27Attene,_Signor_di_Thebe,_et_Coranto,_et_altri_luoghi_Mori_nel_MCCCLXXXXIII_-_Fanelli_Francesco_-_1695.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
